@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — a statement examined on the day it was written, at the place
# its own falsifier named
#
# pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn was written on 2026-09-21 and carries a
# falsifier that names three addresses as the likeliest place for it to break:
# the scriptural prohibitions of the Hebrew Bible, the tradition against the
# ill omen of the month of Safar, and the treatment of nakshatra prognostication
# among the low arts in the first discourse of the Digha. Two of the three were
# opened in the same run. Both deviate, and both deviate in the same direction,
# which is not the direction the falsifier predicted.
#
# The falsifier expected a refusal from outside grounded in doctrine alone. It
# found two refusals from outside grounded in neither doctrine nor case: one in
# the refuser's own rule of livelihood, one in a rule of conduct for the
# perplexed. Neither says anything about whether the art works. The statement
# as worded is about what a refusal rests on, and it offers two rests; the
# holding now carries two refusals that rest on a third thing, and a third
# thing that is not a claim about the world at all.
#
# The protocol therefore stands at cases-with-deviation and its recommendation
# is written out below rather than carried back into the node, following the
# practice of the protocol before it.
#

pmp:RefusalGroundExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a refusal of the calendar rests on a case or on a state of the practitioner, or on neither"@en, "Ob eine Zurückweisung des Kalenders auf einem Fall oder auf einem Zustand des Übenden ruht, oder auf keinem von beiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a transmission rejecting the choosing of a day from outside the practice grounds the rejection in a case, and that one rejecting it from inside grounds it in a state of the practitioner. The population is every node in the compiled holding whose wording rejects the choosing of a day, together with the three addresses the claim's own falsifier names as unread. The six nodes already in the holding were classified when the claim was formed; this protocol opened two of the three named addresses at the wording and classified what it found."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that rejects the choosing of a day from outside the practice and grounds the rejection in doctrine alone, adducing no occasion; or one that rejects it from inside and does adduce an occasion. Neither was met. What was met instead was a third ground the claim has no room for, in two of the two addresses opened: the refuser's own rule of livelihood, and a rule of conduct for one already perplexed by an omen. The claim is not refuted by these, because it states what a refusal rests on and these do not rest on either of its two grounds; it is shown to be a statement about a narrower class than the one it appears to describe."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two corpus searches were carried, each recorded as its own node. The first read the whole of the Mahasila of the first discourse of the Digha in Rhys Davids's English and asked what ground the discourse gives for holding aloof from the choosing of days; the answer is the recurring clause wrong means of livelihood and nothing else, and the passage became pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt. The second searched a nineteenth-century compiler's whole text for the prophetic traditions on omens and found the entries FA'L and DIVINATION, which yielded pmc:SunnaTiyaraLaTamnaka; the same search for the tradition against the ill omen of the month of Safar came back empty in every edition reachable, and that failure is recorded as its own node with the outcome corpus-unreachable rather than as an absence in the corpus. Not searched: the Hebrew Bible, which is the third address the falsifier names and which this holding does not carry as a transmission at all. That is the largest thing this protocol did not do."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "The first deviation. The Mahasila runs seven paragraphs of one shape — whereas some recluses and brahmans, while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood, by low arts, such as these, and then a list, and then Gotama the recluse holds aloof from such low arts. The choosing of days stands in two of the seven lists, among some two hundred items that include palmistry, snake charming and bringing forth flames from one's mouth. The discourse never says that the days do not fall out as the almanac has them. Its ground is that one who lives on the food of the faithful does not earn by this, which is a rule about the refuser and not a judgement on the art."@en ;
    skos:note "The second deviation, and it is the sharper of the two because it concedes what it refuses. Asked what to do about the bad omens he had taken in the time of ignorance, the Prophet is reported as answering: if from a bad omen you are thrown into perplexity, let it not hinder you from doing the work you had intended to do. The perplexity is granted. What is refused is that it should govern the act. That is neither an occasion nor an exemption but an instruction, and beside it in the same compiler stands the outright prohibition of diviners with a mechanism attached — one of the jinn steals away the truth and carries it into the diviner's ear — which shows that the transmission argues when it chooses to and declines to here."@en ;
    skos:note "Why both count as deviations rather than as confirmations of the outside half. The claim's outside half says the refusal is grounded in a case: an occasion on which one date carried two outcomes, or one outcome many dates, or the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost what he sought. Neither of these two adduces any occasion. They also do not do what the falsifier expected, which was to ground the refusal in doctrine; a doctrine at least asserts something about the world, and neither of these asserts anything about the world. The right description of both is abstention, and the holding did not have the word."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction this protocol recommends. The claim should be restated over a narrower population and the third ground should be named beside the other two. First, over transmissions that dispute the art: those that dispute it from outside argue from an occasion, and this survives with the three witnesses it was built on and no exception found. Second, over transmissions that release a practitioner from an art they otherwise teach: they ground the release in a state of the practitioner, and this too survives with its three. Third, and new: a transmission may decline the art without disputing or teaching it, and then it grounds the declining in a rule of its own — of livelihood, or of how to act when already perplexed — and asserts nothing at all about whether the art works. Two witnesses in two spheres, both read on the day of this protocol."@en ;
    skos:note "What this protocol does not establish, stated plainly because the finding is fresh and thin. Two addresses out of three, one of them through a compiler and not at the wording of the source; the third address untouched and the transmission it belongs to absent from this holding. Two witnesses are enough to show that the claim's two grounds are not exhaustive and not nearly enough to say how the third ground distributes. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher because both deviating cases were found by this run's own reading rather than by a search of the assembled holding, and a run that finds its own counter-cases has not yet been checked by anyone."@en .

pmp:BrahmajalaLowArtsGroundSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "What ground the first discourse gives for holding aloof from the choosing of days"@en, "Welchen Grund die erste Lehrrede dafür angibt, sich des Wählens der Tage zu enthalten"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Digha Nikaya 1, Brahmajala Sutta, the Cula-, Majjhima- and Mahasila in full, paragraphs 8 to 27, DN i.4-12, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 4-26, read continuously; the remainder of the discourse, on the sixty-two views, was read only as far as was needed to fix where the paragraphs on conduct end" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any ground at all, anywhere in the seven paragraphs of the low arts, for holding aloof from the arranging of lucky days and the foretelling by constellations: a statement that the art does not work, an occasion on which it failed, a cosmology that rules it out, an exemption granted to the attained, or a reason of any other kind beside the recurring clause about livelihood. The question decides whether this passage falsifies pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn, which names it as a likely counter-case." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the volume for: lucky (inspected at every hit), unlucky, nakshatra, constellation, auspicious, low arts (the seven framing sentences read in full), wrong means of livelihood, holds aloof (all occurrences inspected), and the translator's notes at every one of the paragraphs in question. The lists at paragraphs 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26 and 27 were read item by item." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "No ground of any of the kinds looked for. The clause is the same in all seven paragraphs and it is about earning: while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood. The choosing of days is refused as a trade and the discourse says nothing about whether the trade delivers what it promises."@en ;
    skos:note "One place in the same volume argues instead of listing, and it is not in this discourse. The translator's note at the paragraph on clothes gnawed by mice points to a birth story that laughs the superstition out of court, and another note points to a birth story that mocks the interpreters of dreams. The corpus therefore has the argumentative form available and puts it in its narrative literature, which is exactly where the forty-ninth birth story stands. The doctrinal text lists; the story argues."@en ;
    skos:note "Limits. One discourse in one translation of 1899, and the commentary of Buddhaghosa, which the translator quotes constantly in his notes, was not read as a text of its own."@en .

pmp:SafarOmenSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the tradition against the ill omen of Safar is reachable in an edition this holding may use"@en, "Ob die Überlieferung gegen das Vorzeichen des Safar in einer hier verwendbaren Ausgabe erreichbar ist"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Thomas Patrick Hughes, A Dictionary of Islam, London: W. H. Allen 1885, whole text, some four and a half million characters; plus a search of the open scan archive for a public-domain English rendering of the canonical collections or of the Mishkat al-Masabih" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The tradition in which the Prophet denies contagion, the bird of ill omen, the owl and the ill omen of the month of Safar, in any English version that a CC0 holding may quote. The claim's falsifier names this tradition as one of three places where pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn is likeliest to break, so its wording matters and a paraphrase from memory would not do." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of the dictionary for: Safar (12 hits, every one inspected, all of them about the month as a calendar unit or about the feast of its last Wednesday), evil omen, no infection, contagion, bird of ill, omens, augury, auguries, lucky and unlucky. In the scan archive: Mishkat-ul-Masabih English translation Matthews; Sahih Bukhari English translation; A Manual of Hadith. The two scans that resolved carried no usable text." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "The outcome is about the access path and not about the transmission. The tradition is well known to stand in the canonical collections; what could not be reached is a rendering of it that this holding may reproduce. The standard English versions are in copyright, the early nineteenth-century translation of the Mishkat did not resolve in the archive, and the 1885 compiler who does print many traditions on omens does not print this one under any of the words searched."@en ;
    skos:note "What the same search did find, and it is why the run did not come away empty. The dictionary's entries on omens and on divination print three sayings and one exchange on exactly the question at issue, and the exchange became pmc:SunnaTiyaraLaTamnaka. A refusal of the omen was therefore recovered from this sphere; it is simply not the one the falsifier named."@en ;
    skos:note "This is an exhibited gap of the access kind and it should be closed by anyone who can reach a quotable text. Until then no node in this holding may be cited as carrying the wording of that tradition, and none does."@en ;
    skos:note "The outcome is set at corpus-unreachable and it is weaker than the term sounds, because the first stage of this search was thin and the run says so rather than letting the state stand unqualified. No survey was made of which English renderings of the canonical collections are out of copyright at all; three identifiers were tried in the open scan archive on the strength of names already known, and when they failed the run went to a compiler it happened to remember. A proper landscape stage would ask the question the other way round — which renderings may be reproduced, and which of them index the traditions on omens — and it was not asked. Anyone reopening this should treat the state as not reached rather than as not reachable."@en .
